<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zhu Wen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">852–912 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zhu Wen was a Chinese military leader who first fought as a rebel under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huang-chao">Huang Chao</a> and then served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty</a> before turning against it. In 907 he forced the last Tang emperor to abdicate and founded the Later Liang dynasty. This ended the Tang and opened the period of the Five Dynasties and Ten Kingdoms.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zhu-wen.jpg" alt="Zhu Wen" width="320" height="358" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zhu_Wen_(Liang_Taizu).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zhu Wen was born on 5 December 852, the youngest of three sons of Zhu Cheng, an instructor in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/four-books-and-five-classics">Five Classics</a> in Dangshan County. His father died while he was a boy, and his mother took her sons to the household of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-chong">Liu Chong</a> of Xiao County. He formed a bandit gang and in about 877 joined the rebel army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huang-chao">Huang Chao</a>. After Huang Chao took the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> in January 881, Zhu received a separate command and captured Tong Prefecture.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>By 882 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huang-chao">Huang Chao</a> was effectively surrounded. Zhu surrendered to the Tang governor Wang Chongrong. Emperor Xizong of Tang rewarded him with a command and a new name, Quanzhong, wholeheartedly loyal. On 3 May 883 he was appointed military governor of Xuanwu Circuit at Bian Prefecture, the modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a>. In spring 884 he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-keyong">Li Keyong</a>, chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatuo">Shatuo Turks</a>, routed Huang Chao's generals. On the night of 11 June 884 Zhu tried to have Li Keyong assassinated, which began a struggle of forty years between their houses.</p><p>He destroyed rival warlords such as Qin Zongquan, Shi Pu, Zhu Xuan and Zhu Jin. In 902 he besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-maozhen">Li Maozhen</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fengxiang">Fengxiang</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-zhaozong-of-tang">Emperor Zhaozong of Tang</a> was held, and in 903 Li Maozhen handed the emperor over; all the palace <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a> were then slaughtered. In spring 904 Zhu forced the emperor, his household and the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> to move to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>. That autumn he had Emperor Zhaozong murdered and a child, Emperor Ai of Tang, enthroned. Nine of the dead emperor's sons were killed, and some 30 senior officials of aristocratic family were forced to commit suicide at Baima and their bodies thrown into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The deposed Emperor Ai was made Prince of Jiyin and poisoned in 908. Most circuits submitted to the new emperor, but the realms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-keyong">Li Keyong</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-maozhen">Li Maozhen</a>, Yang Wo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-jian-former-shu">Wang Jian</a> did not. A siege of the Jin city of Lu was broken in 908 by Li Keyong's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-cunxu">Li Cunxu</a>, and in spring 911 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/later-liang">Later Liang</a> army was crushed at Boxiang.</p><p>The histories describe him as violent towards his subordinates and say that he raped the wives of his officers and of his own sons. In summer 912, gravely ill, he meant to leave the throne to his adoptive son Zhu Youwen and ordered another son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-yougui">Zhu Yougui</a>, away from the capital. Fearing execution, Zhu Yougui led imperial guards into the palace and killed him on 18 July 912.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zhu Wen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The last Tang emperors were puppets of competing warlords, and the dynasty's formal end came in 907 when <b>Zhu Wen</b>, a former lieutenant of Huang Chao who had defected to the Tang and risen to dominate the court, deposed the last emperor and founded the Later Liang.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The warlord <b>Zhu Wen</b> forced Emperor Ai, a puppet ruler since 904, to abdicate, ending the Tang dynasty after nearly three hundred years.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-12">12 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The warlord <b>Zhu Wen</b>, who had seized control of the imperial government and moved Emperor Zhaozong to Luoyang, had him murdered.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-22">22 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>When <b>Zhu Wen</b> deposed the Tang in 907, Wang Jian refused to recognise the Later Liang and proclaimed himself emperor.</blockquote><a href="/state/former-shu">Former Shu</a></li><li><blockquote>When <b>Zhu Wen</b> ended the Tang and founded the Later Liang in 907, Li Keyong refused to submit.</blockquote><a href="/state/jin-five-dynasties">Jin (Five Dynasties)</a></li><li><blockquote>Gao Jixing, a former slave adopted into a household close to <b>Zhu Wen</b>, was appointed military governor of Jingnan at Jiangling by the Later Liang in 907.</blockquote><a href="/state/jingnan">Jingnan</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Dangshan County, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.43,116.34&amp;year=891" aria-label="Dangshan County on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7508 917 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Zhu Wen"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7958 1187h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7958 1187h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.43,116.34&amp;year=891">What was here in 891 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
